<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Richardson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1689">1689</a>–<a href="/years/1761">1761</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Richardson was an English printer and novelist. His novels Pamela (1740) and Clarissa (1748), told in the form of letters, explored the feelings and moral struggles of their heroines and won a huge readership across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Clarissa, one of the longest novels in English, is regarded as a landmark in the development of the psychological novel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-richardson.jpg" alt="Samuel Richardson" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Highmore · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Richardson_by_Joseph_Highmore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Samuel Richardson was baptised on 19 August 1689 and was probably born in Mackworth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>, one of nine children. He always concealed the exact location of his birth. His father was a joiner who, by Richardson's own account, had come to the notice of James Scott, 1st Duke of Monmouth, and was forced to give up his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> business after the failure of the Monmouth Rebellion. The family later returned to London, and Richardson was probably educated at Christ's Hospital, although accounts of his schooling conflict.</p><p>His father could not afford to train him as a clergyman, so he chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. In 1706, at the age of 17, he was bound in a seven-year apprenticeship to the printer John Wilde in Aldersgate Street, a master said to grudge every hour that did not serve his profit. Richardson later married Wilde's daughter Martha. All six of their children died in infancy or childbirth, and Martha died in childbirth in 1731. In 1733 he married Elizabeth Leake, the daughter of the printer John Leake, and four of their six children, all daughters, reached adulthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Richardson's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> business prospered. He printed the Daily Journal between 1736 and 1737 and the Daily Gazetteer in 1738, and in all printed almost 500 works, working periodically with the bookseller Andrew Millar. In 1739 the booksellers Charles Rivington and John Osborn asked him for a volume of model letters for country readers who could not write for themselves. While writing it he was inspired to attempt a story, and on 6 November 1740 he published Pamela; or, Virtue Rewarded, his first novel, at the age of 51. It was sometimes regarded as the first novel in English.</p><p>Pamela was followed by sequels, and then by Clarissa; or, The History of a Young Lady, which was not complete to his satisfaction until October 1746. Readers whom he asked to help shorten it wanted it kept entire. His third and last novel, The History of Sir Charles Grandison, appeared in 1753. Richardson became Master of the Stationers' Company and counted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-johnson">Samuel Johnson</a>, Sarah Fielding, the physician George Cheyne and the theologian William Law among his acquaintances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>All three novels are epistolary, told in letters. Richardson was a tireless letter writer and had faith that letters could portray character accurately. The form let him give distinct characters a voice directly to the reader and offered multiple perspectives. He hoped, however, to lead readers to interpret the text in the ways he chose, towards moral regeneration, and Susan Whyman writes that his goal was to reform reading practices and lives as well. His one major later work was a collection of the moral sentiments of his three novels. He died in July 1761 after an attack of apoplexy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Richardson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…after the union: David Hume, Adam Smith, whose Wealth of Nations appeared in 1776, and Adam Ferguson shaped modern philosophy, economics and social science, while Samuel Johnson’s Dictionary (1755) and the novels of Daniel Defoe, <b>Samuel Richardson</b> and Henry Fielding helped establish modern English prose and the novel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1735" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Samuel Richardson"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1735">What was here in 1735? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But, O sir! my soul is of equal importance with the soul of a princess; though my quality is inferior to that of the meanest slave.&#8221;</p><footer>Pamela, or Virtue Rewarded (1740) &#183; original &#183; spoken by Pamela to her master, Mr. B. &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6124/pg6124-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
